EXT. DRY CREEK BED - NOON

Heat. Cicadas. A dead mule.

JEB HARLAN, 40s, crouches behind it, rifle up. Across the gully, CYRUS DOYLE, 40s, flat on his belly in the sage. Both unshaven. Both in gray hats.

                    RUTHIE (O.S.)
          Boys! Y'all dead yet?

Jeb flinches. Cyrus doesn't.

                    JEB
          Ruthie, stay in the wagon!

                    RUTHIE (O.S.)
          Wagon's on fire!

Silence. Smoke rolls over the ridge.

He stands, then drops. A shot kicks dust off the mule. He fires back. Nothing moves in the sage.

                    CYRUS
          (hoarse)
          You hit?

                    JEB
          No. You?

                    CYRUS
          Hell if I know.

He crawls to the lip of the bank. He sees the other one's hat. He grins, ugly.

                    JEB
          That's my hat.

                    CYRUS
          It was on the ground.

Another shot. Both duck. The mule groans, then settles.

                    RUTHIE (O.S.)
          Cyrus! He's lying. Jeb never fired a round!

He turns his rifle toward Ruthie's voice. The other one's barrel follows his. They hold like that, sweat running, the smoke thickening.
